{agreed}Before anyone proposes anything for {title}: {ask}

Two outcomes everyone rates the same way:

bad: {bad}
ideal: {ideal}

How good would each be for your role? Rate each from 0 to 100 on your own scale, one line each, with why:

bad: <0-100> - <why>
ideal: <0-100> - <why>

Your ideal has to rate higher than the bad outcome. Every rating you give later is placed between these two, so it means the same as everyone else's.

{agreed}{ask}

{bar_said}

Propose the one option you think best serves your role for {title}: the concrete terms in one or two sentences, then a line saying why, with the facts behind it:

<the option>
why: <the reasons>

Don't hedge or pre-compromise; everyone rates every option next.

The options for {title}:

{options}

{bar_said}

Rate each option for your role, on the scale you rated the two outcomes on, one line each with your main reason or concern in one short sentence:

{rating_lines}

Keep the option's name in brackets, so the thread can follow: `B (Ship on Friday): 40 - the Friday window breaks certification`. The reason matters more than the number: it's what the others will have to answer.

The best option so far for {title} is:

{pick}

How everyone sees it:

{standing}

{shortfall}

Propose something new that answers these objections, not a small change to it:

{objections}

{bar_said} Aim for an option you'd rate near your ideal that the others can still accept. Reply with the option, then a line saying what it answers and how:

<the option>
why: <what it answers>

Every option so far:

{options}

A new option for {title}:

{new_options}

Rate it the same way, one line with your reason in one short sentence:

{new_rating_lines}

